  "concepts": [
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0231#4", "p0231#5", "p0231#6"],
      "solution": "Throughout, real exponents and real logarithms are meant. With a>0 and a!=1, f(x)=a^x has domain R and range (0,+infinity). It is continuous and strictly increasing for a>1, strictly decreasing for 0<a<1. For a>1 its limits at -infinity and +infinity are 0 and +infinity respectively; for 0<a<1 these limits are reversed. Thus a^x=b has exactly one real solution for every b>0, and none for b<=0. Positivity excludes zero and negative b; continuity and the stated limits give existence for every positive b; strict monotonicity gives uniqueness."
    },
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0231#7", "p0231#8", "p0231#10"],
      "solution": "Textual mathematical invariant only, without inspecting or approving Figure 83: for a>1, a horizontal line y=b intersects y=a^x once if b>0 and never if b<=0. The intersection abscissa is log_a(b). If b>1 it is positive, if b=1 it is zero, and if 0<b<1 it is negative. y=0 is an asymptote, never an attained point. No printed grid dimensions, figure labels, or drawing values are inferred from this invariant."
    },
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0231#9", "p0232#1", "p0232#3", "p0232#4", "p0232#7"],
      "solution": "For a>0, a!=1, b>0, log_a(b) is the unique real exponent c satisfying a^c=b; equivalently log_a(b)=c iff a^c=b. The logarithmic function x->log_a(x) has domain (0,+infinity), range R, and is the inverse of x->a^x. Its graph contains (1,0) and (a,1), has vertical asymptote x=0, and is increasing for a>1 and decreasing for 0<a<1. Neither asymptote nor an infinite endpoint is included. The assembly definition at p0232#3 literally has a>0, a!=0, unlike the correct a!=1 elsewhere. The redundant a!=0 does not exclude a=1: 1^c=1 for every c and 1^c=b has no solution for b!=1. Consequently it cannot define a unique real logarithm. The sole authorized modern correction is a!=0 -> a!=1 under erratum p0232-math-1; its actual modern provenance binding remains to be checked only after this freeze. No other source mathematics is to be changed."
    },
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0232#2", "p0232#5", "p0232#6"],
      "solution": "3^4=81, so log_3(81)=4. 10^(-3)=1/1000=0.001, so log_10(0.001)=-3. 2^3=8, so log_2(8)=3. 3^(-4)=1/81, so log_3(1/81)=-4. 5^0=1, so log_5(1)=0. Neither 3^x=-27 nor 2^x=0 has a real solution, so log_3(-27) and log_2(0) are undefined over R."
    },
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0232#8", "p0232#9", "p0232#10"],
      "solution": "Example 1: log_2(b)=-5 implies b=2^(-5)=1/32, which is positive and hence in the logarithm's domain. Conversely 2^(-5)=1/32 verifies the value."
    },
    {
      "sourceRefs": ["p0232#11", "p0232#12", "p0232#13"],
      "solution": "Example 2: let x=log_sqrt(5)(125). The base sqrt(5)>1 and argument 125>0 are admissible. (sqrt(5))^x=125 becomes 5^(x/2)=5^3, giving x/2=3 and x=6 by injectivity of the exponential. Conversely (sqrt(5))^6=5^3=125."
    }
  ],
