    {
      "number": 731,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0234#2"],
      "steps": [
        "The old interval notation [0,+infinity[ means [0,+infinity), including 0 and excluding infinity.",
        "f(x)=2x+3 for x>=0 has domain [0,+infinity). Its minimum is f(0)=3 and it increases without bound, so its range is [3,+infinity).",
        "Its graph is the ray on y=2x+3 starting with a filled point at (0,3), passing through (1,5) and (2,7), extending up and right. The line's points with x<0, including (-3/2,0), are not part of the graph.",
        "The opposite/inverse relation g reverses ordered pairs: x=2y+3 with y>=0. Equivalently y=(x-3)/2 with x>=3.",
        "Thus the domain of g is the range of f, [3,+infinity), and the range of g is the domain of f, [0,+infinity). In this example g is also a function because f is one-to-one.",
        "The graph of g is the ray on y=(x-3)/2 starting with a filled point at (3,0), passing through (5,1) and (7,2), extending up and right with slope 1/2. The points with x<3, including (0,-3/2), are excluded.",
        "The two rays are reflections across y=x. Solving their supporting-line equations 2x+3=(x-3)/2 gives 4x+6=x-3, hence x=-3 and y=-3. This point is excluded by both domain restrictions, so the two restricted rays have no intersection."
      ],
      "answer": {
        "f": {"equation": "y=2x+3, x>=0", "domain": "[0,+infinity)", "range": "[3,+infinity)", "endpoint": "(0,3), filled"},
        "g": {"equation": "x=2y+3, y>=0; equivalently y=(x-3)/2, x>=3", "domain": "[3,+infinity)", "range": "[0,+infinity)", "endpoint": "(3,0), filled"}
      }
    }
